  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 130 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms \nwith very heavy rainfall will continue be possible today and \nTuesday, and could result in an isolated flooding threat.\n\n\ufeffCentral NC remains situated between a weak upper-level low that has \nsettled near the Southern Appalachians and the sub-tropical ridge \noff the East Coast, with moist southwesterly flow, precipitable \nwater (PW) values near or above two inches, and muggy dew points in \nthe mid-to-upper 70s. Similar to today, weak diffluence aloft and \nsubtle perturbations in the southwesterly flow should support \nscattered to, at times, numerous showers and isolated thunderstorms \nfrom late this morning through this evening. Most guidance suggests \nactivity will be focused near or east of I-95 in the deep moisture \naxis, or along a cold front/convergence zone shifting east into the \nPiedmont as the upper low moves across northern Georgia. Activity \nshould increase with peak heating, as elevated dew points support \nthe redevelopment of 500-1000 MLCAPE, despite weak lapse rates \naloft. Rainfall rates of 1-2 inches per hour and the potential for \ntraining could lead to isolated flooding, but overall QPF and \nconfidence in any specific area remain too low to warrant a Flood \nWatch, despite the generally favorable pattern.\n\n\nOn Tuesday, the upper low is expected to wobble slightly back to the \nnorthwest, and a more southeasterly flow will likely bring a surge \nof moisture with PW values exceeding 2 inches back across the \nPiedmont. Deep-layer steering flow is rather weak, and MBE velocity \nis also very low on the northeast side of the low. Consequently, \nthere may be a slightly higher risk of flooding given the potential \nfor greater coverage of showers and storms, combined with their slow \nmovement.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Mainly diurnally driven showers and storms expected \nfrom Wednesday into next weekend.\n\nThe upper low over the Southeast U.S. is forecast to drift northward \nand be absorbed by a trough crossing the Great Lakes by the end of \nthe week. With the Bermuda High remaining in place, a moist \nenvironment will persist over central North Carolina, though with \ndecreased forcing outside of diurnal influences such as differential \nheating, sea breezes, and other boundaries, especially toward the \nend of the week. it will remain warm and muggy, with highs in the \n80s and dew points in the 70s, resulting in weak instability each \nday and isolated to scattered afternoon showers and storms.",
